<section class="section">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-node">
      <strong>A todo</strong>
      <span>Added by you, an MCP client, or a draft talked over with an agent.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-node flow-key">
      <strong>A lane with an agent</strong>
      <span>When the todo is ready there, the runner starts a run: the agent's model, its instructions, its MCP servers.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-node">
      <strong>A run</strong>
      <span>Logs every tool call and records what it made. It can split the todo into new ones it then waits on.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-node flow-routes">
      <strong>The lane's route</strong>
      <span><code class="pass">PASS</code> moves the todo along the success route, or archives it.</span>
      <span><code class="fail">FAIL</code> sends it along the failure route.</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-caption">
    Chain lanes and you have a pipeline: a Planner splits a request into todos,
    a Worker does each one, and a Reviewer rules PASS or FAIL, sending the work
    back when it is not done. With auto-run off, nothing starts until you press
    <strong>Run now</strong>.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section cta-final">
  <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
  <p>One container plus Postgres: a compose file, two secrets, and <code>docker compose up</code>. Nothing to clone, nothing to build.</p>
  <p class="cta">
    <a class="button" href="{{ '/get-started/' | url }}">Get started</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}">Read the docs</a>
  </p>
</section>

